WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.772
This architecture diagram helps separate responsibilities.

00:00:03.772 --> 00:00:06.722
At the bottom are the actual capabilities.

00:00:06.722 --> 00:00:10.742
A local Q W E N model generates language.

00:00:10.742 --> 00:00:13.607
Chroma stores and retrieves vectors.

00:00:13.607 --> 00:00:16.847
Duck Duck Go can provide public web search.

00:00:16.847 --> 00:00:21.393
S Q L or an A P I can return exact structured facts.

00:00:22.011 --> 00:00:26.133
Lang Chain sits above those components as an integration layer.

00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:32.803
It provides common ways to represent documents, retrievers, tools, prompts, and models.

00:00:32.803 --> 00:00:38.468
Lang Graph sits above that as the orchestration layer.

00:00:38.468 --> 00:00:42.898
It controls shared state and transitions between steps.

00:00:42.898 --> 00:00:46.423
Then your actual application sits at the top.

00:00:46.423 --> 00:00:53.994
That might be a support assistant, a research assistant, a health information workflow, or a class project.

00:00:54.444 --> 00:00:56.966
This separation is useful when debugging.

00:00:56.966 --> 00:01:00.381
If retrieval is wrong, inspect the retriever.

00:01:00.381 --> 00:01:03.886
If tool selection is wrong, inspect routing.

00:01:03.886 --> 00:01:07.726
If the graph loops, inspect state and edges.

00:01:07.726 --> 00:01:11.283
Do not blame the language model for every failure in the system.
